\strong{Status text and bubbles.} Use the always visible status line for a short
description of the current state.
Use a bubble for a message about something that happened; it may time out.
Use \code{mascot_bubble(..., duration = Inf)} for a message the user must act on,
such as an invalid form, because a timed message can vanish before it is read.
Bubbles survive mood changes and swaps; a queued bubble's timer starts when it
appears. See \code{\link[=mascot_bubble]{mascot_bubble()}}
for timing and closing behaviour.

\strong{Shiny modules.} In a module, use \code{mascot(ns("helper"))} in the UI and
\code{update_mascot("helper")} in its server. A \code{session$ns("helper")} id is also
accepted, including in nested modules. The session namespace is added once.
An invalid \code{id} type, missing session or extra argument is an error. Calling
with all update arguments \code{NULL} does nothing.

\strong{Gestures.} A character or mood change applies before the gesture. During a
character swap the gesture is skipped, because the wave within the swap
replaces it. Reduced motion skips movement. The latest gesture sent before the
mascot appears plays once on appearance, after its queued character and mood
are applied.

\strong{React to your app's state.} Call \code{update_mascot()} from an observer. Shiny
sends updates when the observer finishes, so for a mood to show while a long
computation runs, run it as a \link[shiny:ExtendedTask]{shiny::ExtendedTask} and follow its status:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{task <- ExtendedTask$new(function(x) promises::future_promise(fit_model(x)))
observeEvent(input$run, \{
  update_mascot("helper", mood = "thinking", status = "Fitting the model")
  task$invoke(data())
\})
observe(\{
  switch(task$status(),
    success = update_mascot("helper", mood = "happy", status = "Model ready"),
    error = update_mascot("helper", mood = "worried", status = "The model failed")
  )
\})
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

For form validation, observe whether the inputs are valid (yourself, or with a
validation package such as \code{shinyvalidate}) and set the mood from it:

